        "2": {
          "id": "2",
          "name": "vision.md",
          "contents": "I want a page for my school's photography club. Make it colorful so it stands out and gets people excited to join.\n\nThe page should have the club name at the top, a short description of what the club does, and a list of upcoming meeting times.",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        }
      },
      "openFiles": [
        "1",
        "2"
      ]
    },
    "offer_browser_tts": "true",
    "teacher_markdown": "**Intent:** This is the first level where students edit code with their own hands. The scope is deliberately tiny: five CSS rules, and only their color values need to change. Students discover that the code they've been reading is editable, that the preview responds instantly, and that nothing breaks permanently. AI Tutor is restricted to explaining - it will not write code here - so the edit-observe loop belongs entirely to the student.\r\n\r\n**Circulation questions:**\r\n\r\n- \"Which rule did you change first? How did you find it?\"\r\n- \"What colors did you pick, and why do they work together when the original ones didn't?\"\r\n- \"Was editing it yourself faster or slower than prompting AI would have been?\"\r\n\r\n**Expected mistakes and misconceptions:**\r\n\r\n- Students may be afraid of breaking the page - reassure them: change one value, look at the preview, change it back if needed\r\n- Students may ask AI Tutor to make the change and be surprised it declines - point them to the instructions: this level is theirs to type\r\n- Students may change colors randomly - ask \"what feeling is Maya going for? Pick colors on purpose\"\r\n",
    "require_edit_to_continue": "true"
  },
